          "template": "rw_passage",
          "passage": "Popular accounts often claim that growing up bilingual gives children a powerful, general boost in mental control that lasts a lifetime, but this idea deserves scrutiny. Consider a large 2015 review by Kenneth Paap and colleagues, who examined dozens of experiments comparing bilingual and monolingual participants on tasks measuring atâ€ tention and self-control. Early, smaller studies had frequently reported a bilingual advantage, and those results were widely publicized. Yet when Paap's team pooled the evidence, the supposed adâ€ vantage all but disappeared: most well-designed studies showed no reliable difference between the groups, and the positive findings tended to come from studies with very few participants. The patâ€ tern suggests that a striking claim can spread widely before the accumulated evidence is weighed carefully.",
